-----------------------------------------------------------------------
- Media Gallery Plugin for Geeklog
- development branch: 1.8.0
- based on: 1.7.3
- license: GPLv2
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Status
------
This branch is the active development line for MediaGallery 1.8.0.
Do not deploy it to production without testing and a full backup.

See ROADMAP.md for the implementation plan and compatibility policy.

Goals for 1.8.0
---------------
MediaGallery 1.8.0 modernizes the plugin while preserving upgrade safety for existing installations.

Main goals:

* keep upgrade compatibility with existing 1.7.x installations;
* require Geeklog 2.1.1 or newer;
* support standard single-site and shared-code multisite deployments with persistent per-site media storage;
* keep administrator preferences in the Geeklog Configuration API where appropriate;
* modernize templates, JavaScript dependencies, accessibility and SEO where needed;
* retain current bug fixes and improve PHP 8.x compatibility;
* improve interoperability with other Geeklog plugins through public plugin services;
* use Geeklog-native mail transport for MediaGallery notifications.

Image-processing requirement
----------------------------
Image uploads require a working image-processing backend because MediaGallery generates thumbnails and display images. PDF and ZIP uploads do not require image conversion and may still work when the image backend is unavailable.

MediaGallery uses Geeklog's configured `$_CONF['image_lib']` backend:

* `gdlib`: the PHP GD extension must be installed and enabled;
* `imagemagick`: the ImageMagick `identify` and `convert` commands must be executable;
* `netpbm`: the configured NetPBM tools must be executable.

MediaGallery 1.8.0 checks the configured backend before resize, conversion, rotation and watermark operations. If the backend is unavailable, MediaGallery returns a clear error instead of reaching an undefined image-processing function.

Persistent media storage
------------------------
MediaGallery 1.8.0 stores public media below Geeklog's image root instead of inside the replaceable plugin directory.

Standard single-site installations use:

    $_CONF['path_images'] . 'mediagallery/'

with the public URL:

    {site_url}/images/mediagallery

When `$_CONF['images_url']` is explicitly defined, MediaGallery uses it as the public image URL root. This is the recommended multisite setup, for example:

    $_CONF['path_images'] = $_CONF['path'] . 'public_html/images/example/';
    $_CONF['images_url']  = $_CONF['site_url'] . '/images/example';

which produces:

    .../public_html/images/example/mediagallery/
    https://example.com/images/example/mediagallery

The historical `public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/` directory remains a migration source only. It is no longer the 1.8 runtime storage location. This is necessary because Geeklog's native plugin ZIP uploader replaces the complete `public_html/mediagallery/` directory during an update.

Mandatory preflight when upgrading 1.7.x with Geeklog's ZIP uploader
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Geeklog removes the old plugin public directory before it loads the new plugin upgrade code. Therefore a 1.7.x site that still stores media below `public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/` must migrate those files before uploading the 1.8.0 ZIP.

Extract the 1.8.0 archive on the server and run:

    php tools/migrate-media-storage.php /path/to/geeklog

The tool copies files to the persistent images location, verifies every source file by relative path and size, and deliberately leaves the legacy source untouched. Only after it reports `Migration OK` should the 1.8.0 ZIP be uploaded through Geeklog.

Fresh 1.8.0 installations seed the same persistent storage automatically. Once a site uses `images/.../mediagallery/`, subsequent plugin ZIP updates cannot remove user media.

Configuration
-------------
Recent MediaGallery versions no longer use the historical manually edited plugin `config.php` file.

MediaGallery 1.8.0 continues this direction. Runtime-derived values such as plugin URLs, template paths and media-storage paths stay outside administrator-editable Configuration API settings.

Obsolete fresh-install settings related only to legacy technologies such as Flash/FlowPlayer have been removed. Old upgraded installations may temporarily retain harmless obsolete rows until their deletion path has been validated on all supported Geeklog versions.

Upgrade policy
--------------
Before upgrading:

* back up the Geeklog database;
* back up MediaGallery media files;
* record any local modifications;
* test the upgrade on a staging or local installation first;
* for a 1.7.x site using the historical media directory, run the pre-migration tool before using Geeklog's ZIP plugin uploader.

The 1.8.0 upgrade preserves existing configuration values, adds only missing Configuration API settings, and migrates legacy media by copy-and-verify semantics without deleting the source.

Media directories
-----------------
Historical 1.7.x installations may contain:

    public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/covers
    public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/disp
    public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/orig
    public_html/mediagallery/mediaobjects/tn

MediaGallery 1.8.0 uses the same internal layout below Geeklog's persistent image path, for example:

    public_html/images/mediagallery/covers
    public_html/images/mediagallery/disp
    public_html/images/mediagallery/orig
    public_html/images/mediagallery/tn

A multisite deployment can use a site-specific image root such as:

    public_html/images/site-a/mediagallery/

Templates, accessibility and SEO
--------------------------------
The 1.8.0 work includes modernization of MediaGallery `.thtml` templates and associated JavaScript, with HTML5 output, responsive behavior, accessibility improvements, semantic headings, canonical URLs and removal of obsolete playback dependencies where safe.

Interoperability
----------------
MediaGallery 1.8.0 exposes album discovery through Geeklog's native plugin-service convention so other plugins can consume permitted album trees without directly querying `mg_*` tables.

Email notifications
-------------------
Moderator notifications use Geeklog `COM_mail()` transport with HTML and plaintext templates instead of the historical bundled PHPMailer path.

Installable test archive
------------------------
A GitHub Actions workflow named **Build MediaGallery installable archive** builds and validates the current development package when an online test archive is needed.

The branch keeps one fixed test archive:

    dist/mediagallery_1.8.0_2.1.1.zip

The ZIP contains one top-level `mediagallery/` directory and excludes `.github/`, `dist/`, `.gitignore`, repository metadata and build-only directories. The workflow validates archive integrity, required 1.8 migration/storage helpers and Geeklog 2.2.2 filename rules. No separate checksum file is retained in `dist/`.

The archive is intentionally rebuilt only at meaningful test points, not after every source commit.

Compatibility target
--------------------
MediaGallery 1.8.0 requires Geeklog 2.1.1 or newer. Development protects PHP 5.6 compatibility for legacy Geeklog 2.1.1 deployments and also checks PHP 7.4, 8.1 and 8.3 syntax/runtime compatibility as the release is finalized.

For the detailed implementation and release-candidate checklist, see ROADMAP.md and IMPLEMENTATION_NOTES.md.
